#use(std/code)

#section{Media and block elements}::

#code{#image}, #code{#blockquote} and #code{#hr} from #code{std/base}.
#code{#image(url alt: "…")} is inline and #strong{requires} the #code{alt:}
text; #code{#blockquote} and #code{#hr} are block-level (they break the
surrounding paragraph).

#code(lang: "plain")
	Above the rule.

	#blockquote{A short quotation.}

	#blockquote
		A longer quotation spanning
		its own indented block.

	#hr

	Here is #image("cat.png" alt: "a napping cat") shown inline.

	#pagebreak

	Content after a page break.

Live:

Above the rule.

#blockquote{A short quotation.}

#blockquote
	A longer quotation spanning
	its own indented block.

#hr

Here is #image("cat.png" alt: "a napping cat") shown inline.

#pagebreak

Content after a page break.

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: #code{#pagebreak} is a block-level page break: HTML #tt{<div style="break-after: page">}, Typst #code{#pagebreak()}, and a raw HTML block in Markdown (which has no native page break).
	#item: Omitting #code{alt:} is an error, on purpose — a missing alt is invisible to sighted authors while breaking screen readers. For a purely decorative image pass an explicit #code{alt: ""}.
	#item: The function is #code{#blockquote}, not #code{#quote} — #code{quote} is a Racket special form, and a call named #code{quote} would never reach the library.
	#item: #code{#hr} is a zero-argument void element: no args, no body.
